course: politics
lesson: 25
title: Ukraine: refugees, aid, sanctions and children's lives
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When war comes to your town, staying can be deadly, so millions of Ukrainians fled abroad after the full-scale invasion in 2022, many to countries like Poland, Ireland, the UK and Canada, news reports said.
explain: Imagine starting a new school where you hardly speak the language, your gran is far away, and in the evening you log on to your old school as well. That is life for many Ukrainian children, so a seat at your lunch table matters.
explain: Other countries help in three main ways: humanitarian aid (food, medicine, generators), military aid (weapons and training for Ukraine's army, without sending their own soldiers to fight), and sanctions (penalties on Russia's trade and money).
explain: People argue about how much to give, which help is right, and who should pay to rebuild; almost everyone agrees that children like Sofiia deserve a welcome.
explain: Words from the scenes: «canny» (North East England) = quite or nice, «lass» = girl; «sod it» = forget it (rude); «sweet as» (New Zealand) = great; «yinz» (Pittsburgh) = you all; «lowkey» = a bit, «slaps» = is great; «pure mental» (Glasgow) = totally mad; «lush» (Wales) = lovely.
fact: Millions of Ukrainians fled abroad after the 2022 invasion, many to countries like Poland, Ireland, the UK and Canada, and their children joined local schools, news reports said.
fact: The UK's Homes for Ukraine scheme let families offer a spare room to Ukrainians fleeing the war, and hosts got a small payment.
fact: Charities and UN agencies use donations to buy food, medicine, blankets and generators for people in and around Ukraine.
fact: Many countries, including the US, the UK, Canada and EU countries, sent weapons, training and money to Ukraine's army but did not send their own soldiers to fight, news reports said.
fact: After the invasion many countries put sanctions on Russia, such as stopping some trade and freezing Russian money kept abroad so it could not be used, and many companies, including McDonald's in 2022, left Russia.
fact: In the US, the UK and Europe, lawmakers have argued for years about how much money and how many weapons to give Ukraine.
fact: The UN and Ukraine reported that many Ukrainian children were taken to Russia from areas Russia controlled; Russia's government said it moved children to keep them safe.
fact: In 2023 the International Criminal Court, a world court that tries people for the worst crimes, asked for the arrest of Russia's president and another official over the taking of children, it was reported; some children have been brought home, and Ukraine says many thousands are still in Russia.
fact: Some Ukrainian cities, including Kharkiv, built classrooms underground, such as in metro stations, so children could learn safely, it was reported.
fact: Many Ukrainian refugee children go to local schools by day and log on to their Ukrainian school online as well, news reports said.
fact: Ukrainians have been rebuilding homes, bridges and playgrounds, and countries argue about whether Russia's frozen money should pay for rebuilding.
fact: The sunflower is Ukraine's national flower and became a symbol of support and hope around the world.
exercise: Why did millions of Ukrainians leave their country after 2022? | To escape the danger of the war | To go on a very long holiday abroad | Because Ukraine closed all its schools forever | Because they all won a big lottery prize
exercise: What is a refugee? | Someone who had to leave their country to escape danger | Someone who moves abroad for a summer job and then comes home again in the autumn | A tourist who loves travelling | A soldier sent abroad to fight
exercise: Sofiia sits alone at lunch in Finn's class. What is the kind thing to do? | Invite her to sit with you and help her with words | Wait until she speaks perfect English before you talk to her | Leave her alone, because new pupils like to be left by themselves | Tell her to go back to her old school if she is not happy here
exercise: What was the UK's Homes for Ukraine scheme? | A plan letting families host Ukrainians in a spare room | A plan to build toy houses for Ukraine | A plan to send UK families to live in Ukraine and look after the empty houses there | A football tournament for Ukrainian teams
exercise: How does money from Mia's bake sale help people in Ukraine? | Charities use it to buy food, medicine and generators | The cupcakes are posted to Ukraine one by one, in little boxes | The money is buried in the school field for good luck, by law | The coins are melted down to make weapons for the army
exercise: Kai's friend asks if sending weapons is the same as joining the war. What did those countries do? | Sent weapons and training but not their own soldiers to fight | Sent all their own soldiers to fight | Sent nothing at all | Sent the same weapons to both sides
exercise: Why do some people oppose sending weapons to Ukraine? | They say it makes the war last longer and risks a bigger one | They say Ukraine invaded first | They say weapons are free, so it does not matter how many are sent or where they end up | They say Ukraine already has too many soldiers
exercise: What are sanctions? | Penalties such as stopping trade or freezing money | Prizes given to the winning country | Peace talks held on a boat | A kind of military aid
exercise: Which company closed all its restaurants in Russia in 2022? | McDonald's | Every Ukrainian bakery | The United Nations | The Eurovision Song Contest
exercise: What is the argument in the US Congress about Ukraine? | How much money and weapons to give Ukraine | Whether Ukraine exists at all | Whether the US should join Russia | Whether to ban sunflowers
exercise: What did the UN and Ukraine report about some Ukrainian children? | They were taken to Russia from areas Russia controlled | They all moved to Antarctica | They became generals in Russia's army | They were all adopted in Canada
exercise: What did the International Criminal Court do in 2023 over the taking of children? | Asked for the arrest of Russia's president and another official | Gave Russia a medal | Closed down for good | Moved all the children to Africa
exercise: Why did Kharkiv build classrooms in a metro station? | Underground is safer from attacks | Trains make very good teachers for maths | It is warmer down there than on the beach | Metro stations are free to rent forever
exercise: Maksym goes to school in Glasgow and logs on to his Ukrainian school too. Why? | His family wants him to keep his language and lessons in case they go home | His Glasgow school opens only on Mondays | He failed his first school | Two schools are the law in Scotland for every child who speaks more than one language
exercise: What do countries argue about when it comes to rebuilding Ukraine? | Who pays, including whether Russia's frozen money should be used | Whether bridges should be made of chocolate | Whether Ukraine needs any new buildings at all, or should just live in tents from now on | Whether rebuilding is allowed in summer
exercise: Why are sunflowers a symbol of Ukraine? | The sunflower is Ukraine's national flower and stands for hope | Sunflowers were invented in Ukraine last year by its scientists | Sunflowers are banned everywhere else in the world except Ukraine | Sunflowers only grow in Ireland, so Ukraine borrowed them as a sign
test: A boy in Newcastle says: «that new lass Sofiia's canny quiet, she just sits on her own, like.» What is the likeliest reason and the best response? | She is new, far from home and still learning English, so invite her over | She does not want any friends ever | She is quiet because she is in trouble with the teachers, so it is best to stay away from her | She is shy of Irish people only
test: Someone in Bristol grumbles: «why should we give them our spare room, they're not our problem.» What is the fair summary? | Some say hosting helps; others say the government should provide homes; both want people housed | Hosting is forced on every family by law, and anyone with a spare room must take a family or pay a fine | Ukrainians are not allowed to live in the UK | Nobody has hosted anyone
test: Mia's friend in Auckland asks: «sweet as, we made $200, but do they eat the actual cupcakes in Ukraine?» What happens? | Charities turn the money into food, medicine and generators near where they are needed | The cupcakes are frozen and flown over on a special plane, so every child in Ukraine gets one each | The money stays in Auckland | Only the icing is sent
test: A girl in Pittsburgh says: «yinz all think sending weapons is the same as joining the war, right?» Which statement is fair? | Some say helping Ukraine defend itself is right; others say it makes the war longer; senders did not send soldiers to fight | All the senders sent their own armies | No country sent anything | Everyone agrees that sending weapons is wrong, so every country that sent them has now asked for all of them to be sent back
test: What would a big company that obeys new sanctions on Russia probably do? | Stop trading there, like McDonald's did in 2022 | Open twice as many shops in Russia | Become the boss of Russia | Pay Russia double
test: On TV one man barks «we've given Ukraine enough, sod it!» and another says «we have to keep helping!» Which is a point each side makes? | One says the money is needed at home; the other says if invasion works, others may try it | Both say Ukraine doesn't exist | Both want to stop all help to everyone, and only disagree about how loudly to say it on TV | Neither of them cares about prices
test: A friend says: «the children taken to Russia all went home ages ago.» How do you correct it? | Some came back, but Ukraine says many thousands are still in Russia | They all went home in a week | No child was ever taken anywhere | They all moved to Canada
test: A teenager says: «an underground school in a metro? lowkey that slaps.» What is true? | It is there to keep children safe from attacks, not for fun | It was built for fun, not safety | There are no lessons underground | The trains run right through the classrooms, so pupils can get off at their stop
test: Liam in Glasgow says: «Maksym does two schools, that's pure mental.» Why might his family do it? | To keep his Ukrainian language and lessons in case they go home | Because he loves homework much more than playing games with friends | Because Scottish schools finish at 10 a.m. | Because two schools are the law for everyone
test: A news clip shows smiling Ukrainians painting a new playground in a damaged town. What does it show? | Even in war, people rebuild and keep going | The war ended years ago everywhere | Painting playgrounds stops missiles | Only children rebuild towns
test: A girl in Swansea says: «Yana's sunflower painting is lush. Will she ever go home?» What is the fair answer? | Some families have gone home and others plan to stay; both choices are normal | All refugees must go home tomorrow, by order of the government, whether it is safe or not | No refugee may ever go home | Only children may go home
exam: Identify the principal reason for which millions of Ukrainians departed their country after February 2022. | To escape the dangers occasioned by the full-scale invasion | To pursue seasonal employment abroad | Pursuant to a compulsory programme of foreign education imposed by the government | To participate in an international sporting competition
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the sanctions imposed upon Russia? | Penalties restricting trade and freezing assets, intended to impede the financing of the war | Rewards conferred upon Russia in respect of its conduct | A deployment of foreign armies into Russian territory, undertaken with the consent of the Russian government | A programme for hosting Russian families in spare rooms
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning military aid furnished to Ukraine by the United States, the United Kingdom and Canada. | Weapons, training and funds were provided, whereas those states did not dispatch their own soldiers to fight | Those states dispatched their entire armies to the front line, together with all their reserves and equipment | Those states furnished assistance to neither party | Those states furnished identical assistance to both parties
exam: State the action reported to have been taken by the International Criminal Court in 2023 in respect of the removal of Ukrainian children to Russia. | It requested the arrest of the Russian president and another official | It awarded a commendation to the Russian government | It declared that no children had been removed from Ukraine at any time | It ordered all children to be relocated to Africa
